[Event "Kings Gambit: the gambit itself"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/rjyt79qq/3snUMYzJ"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis"]

{ Today we play the Kings Gambit. Throw a pawn away on purpose, and grab the middle of the board while he deals with it. [%cal Ye2e4] } 1. e4 { Your pawn marches to e4 and claims the centre. He has to answer it before he does anything else. } (1. d4 { A fine first move, and it leads to a whole different kind of game. } 1... Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 d5 { That is the queen pawn game, calm and strategic. Today we play the open, swashbuckling kind. }) 1... e5 { He answers in the centre too. Now for the gambit itself: push your f pawn two squares, to f4. [%cal Yf2f4] } 2. f4 { The King's Gambit. You offer this pawn to rip his centre pawn off the board and open the position. [%csl Re5] } (2. Nf3 { A completely sound way to meet e5, and a real weapon on its own. Just not today's adventure. } 2... Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. O-O Nxe4 { That is the Ruy Lopez, one of the deepest openings there is. We want something wilder today. }) (2. Bc4 { A fine developing move, aiming at f7 already. But the bishop is happy to wait one more turn. } 2... Nf6 3. Nc3 Nc6 4. d3 Bb4 { A normal, quiet game, sound in every way and a good deal calmer than the gambit we are learning. }) (2. Nc3 { Also perfectly good, a natural developing move. But pushing the f pawn is the whole idea today. } 2... Nf6 3. Nf3 Nc6 4. Bb5 Nd4 { That is the Four Knights Game, solid and well tested. A good opening, and a different lesson. }) 2... exf4 { He grabs the pawn. The gambit is accepted, and now bring your king knight out to f3. [%cal Yg1f3] } 3. Nf3 { The knight jumps to f3 and covers h4, so no queen check waits for you on that square right now. [%csl Rh4] } (3. Bc4 { The Bishop's Gambit, just as sound as ours. But it lets his queen check you on h4 right away. } 3... c6 4. Nc3 Qh4+ 5. Kf1 d5 { Playable, and White has scored well with it for years. Still, your king can never castle now. }) (3. Nc3 { Also fine, and a natural developing move. But f3, not c3, is where this knight really wants to stand. } 3... Nc6 4. Nf3 g5 5. h4 g4 { A move later you reach the very position we are heading for, just with a knight out on each side. }) (3. d4 { Grabbing the centre looks tempting, but his queen checks first and costs you a whole tempo. } 3... Qh4+ 4. Ke2 Qe7 5. Kf2 Nf6 { Your king has to walk to f2 by hand, and it can never castle. Not the game we want. }) 3... g5 { He plants pawns to hold f4 for good. Poke a hole in that wall: push your rook pawn to h4. [%cal Yh2h4] } 4. h4 { You knock on his pawn wall. If he takes on h4, the h column swings wide open toward his king. [%csl Rg5] } (4. d4 { A very good move too, grabbing the centre while it is free. But we want to crack his wall open. } 4... d6 5. g3 g4 6. Nh4 f3 { Sound, and a slower kind of fight. Our rook pawn push opens things up a lot more quickly. }) (4. Nc3 { Also completely sound, and a quieter way to develop before doing anything sharp. } 4... d6 5. d4 g4 6. Bxf4 gxf3 7. Qxf3 { A genuine piece sac, and not quite sound: Black keeps the better chances even with two pawns back. }) (4. Bc4 { Tempting, the bishop stares at f7 already. But the g pawn keeps rolling forward regardless. } 4... g4 5. O-O gxf3 6. Qxf3 Qf6 { A wild pawn storm, but a knight is a knight: you gave one away with no pawn yet to show for it. }) 4... g4 { His pawn shoves past your knight. Do not let it get taken for nothing: jump it forward to e5. [%cal Yf3e5] } 5. Ne5 { Well posted. Your knight lands on e5, safe from the pawns, and stares straight down at f7. [%cal Ge5f7] } (5. d4 { Danger: his pawn on g4 is about to take your knight, and recapturing only wins the pawn back. } 5... gxf3 6. Qxf3 Nc6 7. c3 d6 { A knight traded for a single pawn. The queen gets the pawn back, but the knight itself is gone for good. }) (5. Ng5 { A bold jump at f7. But a simple pawn push chases it straight back home again. } 5... h6 6. Nxf7 Kxf7 7. d4 f3 { Two pawns for a whole knight, and his king only had to step aside once. Not enough to show for it. }) (5. Ng1 { Home again, safe and sad: two whole moves spent to end up back where the knight started. } 5... d5 6. d4 Bh6 7. e5 c5 { He grabs the centre and keeps his extra pawn too. Retreating cost you the whole plan. }) 1-0


[Event "Kings Gambit: the Kieseritzky main line"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/rjyt79qq/xYOugfFE"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppp1p1p/8/4N3/4PppP/8/PPPP2P1/RNBQKB1R b KQkq - 1 5"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/pppp1p1p/8/4N3/4PppP/8/PPPP2P1/RNBQKB1R_b_KQkq_-_1_5"]

{ Your knight sits deep in his camp on e5. This is his main defence — watch how it fights back against it. }
5... Nf6 { He develops with tempo, aiming his knight straight at your pawn on e4. What do you do about it? } 6. d4 { Nice. The pawn backs your knight up on e5 and claims the whole centre for you in one move. [%csl Gd4,Ge5] } (6. Nxg4 { A good pawn grab on its own merits. But building the big centre with d4 serves you better here. } 6... Nc6 7. Nc3 Nd4 8. Nxf6+ Qxf6 { Material is level and the position is tidy, but your attack has fizzled out completely. }) (6. Bc4 { Perfectly playable too, developing with a threat. But your knight on e5 still stands there alone. } 6... d5 7. exd5 Bd6 8. d4 Nh5 { He gains time attacking your bishop, and his extra pawn on f4 sits there safe and sound. }) (6. Be2 { Too shy a square. Your knight in the middle of the board is still standing there with no support. } 6... Nxe4 7. d4 Ng3 8. Rh2 d6 { His knight forks your rook and bishop from g3, and moving the rook only saves one of them. }) 6... d6 { He kicks your knight. Where does it go so it still does a job for you? } 7. Nd3 { Well spotted. From d3 the knight stays safe and leans right on the pawn you are chasing, f4. [%cal Gd3f4] } (7. Bxf4 { Careful: this lets him trade his own pawn for your whole knight on e5, and that is a bad swap for you. } 7... dxe5 8. Bxe5 Nbd7 9. Bf4 Nh5 { A knight for a single pawn — retreating it with the last move kept all of it safe instead. }) (7. Nxf7 { The classic sacrifice. Here it only buys two pawns for the knight, not a real attack. } 7... Kxf7 8. Bxf4 Be6 9. Qd2 Rg8 { His king tucks in safely and you are simply a piece short for your trouble. }) (7. Nc4 { The knight steps aside, but c4 does nothing useful there at all — a wasted move in a sharp position. } 7... Bh6 8. Nc3 Nh5 9. Bd3 O-O { He castles by hand at his leisure and you have no plan left to follow. }) 7... Nxe4 { He grabs your pawn on e4. What is the cleanest way to win a pawn straight back? } 8. Bxf4 { Lovely. The bishop recaptures the pawn and leans hard on his weak pawn back on d6. [%cal Gf4d6] } (8. Qe2 { Also good — the queen eyes the whole e column. But the bishop wants the f4 square more. } 8... Qe7 9. Bxf4 Nc6 10. c3 Nf6 { Fine for you either way, just one move slower to reach the same pawn-up position we are aiming for. }) (8. Nxf4 { The wrong piece takes back. Your knight leaves the centre and the bishop is still asleep at home. } 8... Be7 9. Bd3 Bxh4+ { The h pawn drops with check. Taking with the bishop was the tidier recapture. }) (8. Nc3 { Solid development, but it lets his knight leap out to g3 with real threats. } 8... Ng3 9. Rh2 Qe7+ 10. Kf2 Bg7 { Your king gets stuck in the middle and the initiative has changed hands. }) 1-0


[Event "Kings Gambit: he hits back with the d pawn"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/rjyt79qq/rbS1fAhY"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4PP2/8/PPPP2PP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq f3 0 2"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4PP2/8/PPPP2PP/RNBQKBNR_b_KQkq_f3_0_2"]

{ Not everyone grabs the f pawn. Some players strike straight back in the centre with their own d pawn instead. }
2... d5 { He skips the pawn grab and strikes in the centre with his own d pawn. Take it, or let his centre stand? } 3. exd5 { Yes, take it. Never let him keep a free pawn and a big centre both at once. } (3. Nf3 { Also excellent, and often the tidier choice — it usually comes back around to our own line anyway. } 3... exf4 4. exd5 Nf6 5. Bc4 Nxd5 { You have simply transposed into our line by a different road — same pawn on d5, same knight jump to come. }) (3. fxe5 { Look again: his queen has a clear road all the way to h4, with nothing in the way. } 3... Qh4+ 4. g3 Qxe4+ 5. Qe2 Qxh1 { Your rook is gone for good. Taking on d5 first avoids the whole mess. }) (3. Nc3 { Playable, but he simply pushes his pawn past yours and cramps your whole position. } 3... d4 4. Nce2 Nc6 5. d3 exf4 { His pawn wedge on d4 is awkward to face for the rest of the game. }) (3. d4 { Grabbing more centre looks tempting, but his pieces come flying out with tempo. } 3... exd4 4. Qxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 Nc6 { Your queen gets chased around the board by his developing pieces, and he ends up ahead in the race. }) 3... exf4 { Now he wins the f pawn back. Which of your pieces should come out first, before his queen finds a check? } 4. Nf3 { Good. The knight comes out and covers h4, so no queen check catches you off guard right now. [%csl Rh4] } (4. Bb5+ { A tempting check, but he simply blocks it and gains a move developing on your bishop. } 4... c6 5. Qe2+ Be7 6. dxc6 bxc6 { His pieces come out swinging with tempo while yours still sit at home. }) (4. d4 { The big centre looks right, but the check on h4 is still sitting there wide open. } 4... Qh4+ 5. Kd2 Qd8 6. Nc3 Nf6 { Your king has had to move by hand and you stand clearly worse for it. }) (4. Qf3 { The queen guards nothing useful stuck out there, and she just gets in her own knight's way. } 4... Nf6 5. Nc3 Bd6 6. Bb5+ c6 { She gets chased around the board while he calmly develops ahead of you. }) (4. Bc4 { Tempting: the bishop stares straight at f7 already. But the check comes first regardless. } 4... Qh4+ 5. Kf1 { Playable, but now you can never castle. Developing the knight first sidesteps all of this. }) 4... Nf6 { He goes after your extra pawn sitting on d5. Can you find a way to hold onto it? } 5. c4 { Yes. The little c pawn props d5 up from behind, and your extra pawn holds for good. [%cal Gc4d5] } (5. Bc4 { A good move too. You hand the pawn back but develop with real speed in return. } 5... c6 6. d4 cxd5 7. Bb5+ Nc6 { A fine game either way, though the extra pawn has gone back to him. }) (5. Bb5+ { Also perfectly good. The check buys you a free moment to tuck your king away. } 5... c6 6. dxc6 Nxc6 7. Nc3 Bb4 { Equal and pleasant, but the c pawn plan keeps the extra pawn for you instead. }) (5. Nc3 { Natural development, but now the pawn sitting on d5 simply falls to him for free. } 5... Nxd5 6. Nxd5 Qxd5 7. d4 Be7 { Your extra pawn is gone and his queen sits comfortably in the middle. }) 1-0


[Event "Kings Gambit: he declines with the bishop"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/rjyt79qq/IRvxW008"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4PP2/8/PPPP2PP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq f3 0 2"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4PP2/8/PPPP2PP/RNBQKBNR_b_KQkq_f3_0_2"]

{ Not everyone grabs pawns. Some players develop instead and dare you to prove the gambit is real. }
2... Bc5 { He turns the pawn down and points his bishop straight at f2 instead. Be careful here. } 3. Nf3 { Right. The knight covers h4, so his bishop has no queen check to team up with there. [%csl Rh4] } (3. fxe5 { Greedy, and it opens a straight road for his queen right into your king. } 3... Qh4+ 4. g3 Qxe4+ 5. Qe2 Qxh1 { Your rook is gone for good. That is exactly why the knight move comes first. }) (3. Nc3 { A good move as well, and every bit as sound as the knight to f3 — just a shade less pointed. } 3... d6 4. Nf3 Nf6 5. Bc4 O-O { You reach much the same kind of position by a slightly different road. }) (3. d3 { Solid, but slow, and the check on h4 is still hanging in the air unanswered. } 3... d6 4. Nc3 a5 5. Nf3 Nf6 { Playable, though he gets a very comfortable game out of your delay. }) 3... d6 { He props his bishop's pawn up with d6. What is your plan against that bishop now? } 4. c3 { Nice. The little pawn move prepares d4 next, and d4 kicks his bishop clean off its post. [%cal Gc3d4] } (4. Nc3 { Also good — plain, honest development, and there is nothing at all wrong with it here. } 4... Nf6 5. Bc4 O-O 6. d3 Nbd7 { A normal game either way. But we want to hit that bishop with a pawn. }) (4. Bc4 { Perfectly playable, and now the two bishops stare each other down across the board. } 4... Nf6 5. Nc3 O-O 6. d3 Nbd7 { Even and quiet. Our pawn plan against his bishop is the sharper choice. }) (4. fxe5 { You can grab this pawn, but it only helps him finish developing for free. } 4... dxe5 5. c3 f5 6. Bc4 Nc6 { Equal, and the whole point of holding onto that gambit pawn on f4 has quietly drained away. }) 4... Nc6 { Knight to c6. Your last move was quietly preparing something — now go ahead and play it. } 5. d4 { There it is. The pawn hits his bishop hard and you claim the whole centre in one stroke. [%cal Gd4c5] } (5. fxe5 { You may take here too, and the position stays roughly level either way. } 5... dxe5 6. b4 f5 7. exf5 Bb6 { Playable, but the centre push was the entire point of playing c3 first. }) (5. b4 { The pawn grabs space and hits the bishop too. Not a bad try at all. } 5... Bb6 6. a4 a5 7. b5 Nce7 { Fine for you here, but your own queen side pawns get loose in return. }) (5. Bb5 { The pin looks natural, but he simply hits back hard with his own f pawn. } 5... f5 6. d4 exd4 7. cxd4 Bb4+ { Messy for both sides, and your bishop had to move twice to get there. }) 5... exd4 { He takes the pawn. Two of your pieces can recapture here — only one of them is the right choice. } 6. cxd4 { Exactly. The c pawn takes back, his bishop is kicked away, and the whole centre belongs to you. [%csl Gd4,Ge4] } (6. Nxd4 { The knight takes back instead, but the c pawn was the one meant for that job. } 6... Nf6 7. Bd3 Qe7 8. Nd2 O-O { His pieces flow out freely and your big pawn centre never quite happens. }) (6. Bc4 { The bishop looks nice out there, but you left the pawn sitting alone on d4. } 6... dxc3 7. Nxc3 Nf6 8. e5 dxe5 { Your big centre has evaporated and he stands the better of the two of you. }) 1-0


[Event "Kings Gambit: the Fischer defence"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/rjyt79qq/dU5DOQyZ"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/8/4Pp2/5N2/PPPP2PP/RNBQKB1R b KQkq - 1 3"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/8/4Pp2/5N2/PPPP2PP/RNBQKB1R_b_KQkq_-_1_3"]

{ No pawn wall today. This is the Fischer defence: he meets your gambit calmly with d6 instead of grabbing more. }
3... d6 { The calm d6, keeping everything solid behind it. There is no weakness yet for you to attack. } 4. Bc4 { Good pick. The bishop flies straight out and glares down the diagonal at f7. [%cal Gc4f7] } (4. d4 { Also strong. You take the whole centre for yourself instead of developing first. } 4... g5 5. Qd3 Bg7 6. g3 Nc6 { A good game too, though the bishop was hoping to reach f7 sooner. }) (4. h4 { Stopping his pawn push early. A perfectly reasonable, more patient try. } 4... Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. d4 Nh5 { Sound, but your bishop is still sitting at home doing nothing at all. }) (4. Nc3 { Fine, but it lets him build his pawn wall with the g pawn undisturbed. } 4... g5 5. Bc4 g4 6. O-O gxf3 7. Qxf3 { A wild gambit here too, and you have given up a whole knight for it. }) 4... h6 { He plays h6, getting ready to push his g pawn next. Can you stop that plan cold? } 5. h4 { Yes. Now if the g pawn ever comes, you take twice on g5 and your rook lands on h8. [%csl Rg5] } (5. d4 { Also good, and very natural. But he still gets his pawn wall up for free either way. } 5... g5 6. h4 Bg7 7. Qd3 Nc6 { Sound. You simply play the rook pawn push one move later instead. }) (5. Nc3 { Fine development on its own. But now the g pawn push arrives with real tempo. } 5... g5 6. h4 Bg7 7. d4 Nc6 { Playable, though a shade less pleasant than stopping the wall first. }) (5. O-O { Castling is safe and sensible. But his pawn wall still goes up completely for free. } 5... g5 6. c3 Nc6 7. d4 Nf6 { He keeps his extra pawn on f4 and his whole wall of pawns, and you have no attack to show for its loss. }) 5... Nf6 { Knight to f6, aiming straight at your e pawn. Whose job is it to guard that pawn now? } 6. Nc3 { Perfect. The knight guards your e pawn on e4, and it eyes the d5 square as a bonus. [%cal Gc3e4] } (6. d4 { Look again: nobody at all is standing guard over your pawn on e4 right now. } 6... Nxe4 7. Qe2 Qe7 8. Bxf4 Nf6 { A whole pawn given away for nothing: the knight to c3 first would have kept it on the board. }) (6. O-O { Castling is nice, but that pawn on e4 is still sitting there completely undefended. } 6... Nh5 7. d4 g5 8. Nc3 Bg7 { He holds firmly onto f4 and you have nothing at all to show for it. }) (6. d3 { Solid, but slow, and it leaves the pawn on f4 sitting there for him to keep. } 6... Nh5 7. Nc3 Be7 8. d4 Nc6 { He is comfortable and a clean pawn up for the whole rest of the game. }) 1-0


[Event "Kings Gambit: the early queen check"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/rjyt79qq/IRuK3zgO"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4PP2/8/PPPP2PP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq f3 0 2"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4PP2/8/PPPP2PP/RNBQKBNR_b_KQkq_f3_0_2"]

{ Sometimes he skips development entirely and throws his queen out early with a check instead. }
2... Qh4+ { Out comes the queen with check. Only two moves answer it legally here — pick the right one. } 3. g3 { The right one. The pawn attacks her directly and wins you a whole move of time. [%cal Gg3h4] } (3. Ke2 { The king has to walk by hand, and your own castling rights are gone for the rest of the game. } 3... Bc5 4. Qe1 Qxf4 5. d3 Qf6 { He sits a pawn up on f4, and your king is stuck wandering around in the open with no shelter left. }) 3... Qe7 { She backs off to e7, standing right in front of his own bishop's best diagonal. Your move. } 4. Nc3 { Good. Develop, and leave the pawn on f4 alone for now — his queen is in her own bishop's way. [%csl Rf8] } (4. d3 { Also good, and very sensible. But developing the knight first is the quicker option here. } 4... d5 5. Nc3 dxe4 6. dxe4 Nc6 { Pleasant for you either way, and his pawn on e4 came right off with a tempo on his own queen along the way. }) (4. Bg2 { Fine too, though it does less useful work right now than bringing the knight out. } 4... d5 5. Nc3 dxe4 6. fxe5 Qxe5 { Still nice for you, just a touch slower getting there than our line. }) (4. c4 { Grabs space, and perfectly playable. But developing your pieces comes first here. } 4... exf4 5. Nc3 fxg3 6. hxg3 d6 { The very same idea arrives anyway, just one move later than before. }) 4... exf4 { At last he takes the f pawn back. What is your strongest pawn push here now? } 5. d4 { Yes. Two pawns wide across the centre, and his queen is stuck behind her own blocked men. [%csl Gd4,Ge4] } (5. gxf4 { You can recapture here too, but it cracks the position open right in front of your own king. } 5... d5 6. Qe2 dxe4 7. Nxe4 Nc6 { Your edge has slipped away. The big central push was the stronger choice. }) (5. Bg2 { A good move too. The bishop takes the long road down the big diagonal instead. } 5... fxg3 6. hxg3 d6 7. d4 g6 { Still comfortable for you, though the pawn push hits harder right away. }) (5. Qf3 { The queen comes out early here. Playable, but she can be chased around by his pieces. } 5... c6 6. gxf4 d5 7. d3 dxe4 { Fine for you, and a good deal messier than the clean centre push. }) 5... fxg3 { He takes on g3, right beside your own king. Which of your pawns should recapture there? } 6. hxg3 { Well spotted. The h column swings wide open and your rook stares straight down it. [%cal Gh1h7] } (6. Bf4 { Also excellent: develop the bishop first and pick up the pawn on g3 a little later. } 6... Nf6 7. hxg3 d6 8. Qd3 Nc6 { Just as good. Both roads open up the same h column for your rook. }) (6. Qf3 { Also fine, but that lonely pawn on g3 really deserves to be captured at once. } 6... d6 7. Nd5 Qd8 8. Bf4 c6 { Still pleasant for you, just a little untidy compared with taking the pawn. }) (6. Nh3 { The knight goes to the rim just to guard the pawn. Too passive a square for it. } 6... Nf6 7. Bg2 gxh2 8. Bg5 c6 { His pawn crawls on to h2 and your whole edge has quietly disappeared. }) 1-0
